input.script:
export const meta = {
  name: 'gdd-sync-foundation',
  description: 'Apply Foundation ADR GDD-sync edits and ADR-wins contradiction fixes, one Sonnet writer per file, then a Sonnet diff verifier',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Write', detail: 'one writer per target file applies its packet', model: 'sonnet' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'one verifier per file reads the diff', model: 'sonnet' },
  ],
}

const ROOT = 'D:/the-synaptic-sea-2d'
const WRITE_SCHEMA = { type: 'object', properties: {
  file: { type: 'string' },
  applied: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { id: { type: 'string' }, where: { type: 'string' }, note: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['id', 'where'] } },
  already_done: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
  objections: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { id: { type: 'string' }, gdd_intent: { type: 'string' }, adr_says: { type: 'string' }, recommendation: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['id', 'gdd_intent', 'adr_says', 'recommendation'] } },
  skipped: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { id: { type: 'string' }, reason: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['id', 'reason'] } },
  registry_requests: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { registry: { type: 'string' }, entry: { type: 'string' }, change: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['registry', 'entry', 'change'] } },
}, required: ['file', 'applied', 'already_done', 'objections', 'skipped', 'registry_requests'] }

const VERIFY_SCHEMA = { type: 'object', properties: {
  file: { type: 'string' },
  ok: { type: 'boolean' },
  problems: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { id: { type: 'string' }, problem: { type: 'string' }, line: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['problem'] } },
  fixed: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
}, required: ['file', 'ok', 'problems', 'fixed'] }

const RULES = `Rules: work in ${ROOT}. Edit ONLY the one target file named below. Never run git (not even status/diff) except where a step explicitly allows a read-only "git diff". Do not edit any registry (design/registry/entities.yaml, docs/architecture/tr-registry.yaml, docs/registry/architecture.yaml): return registry_requests instead. Files are large; a hook refuses whole-file reads over 40 KB, so Grep for anchors and Read ranges with offset/limit; Edit with an exact, unique old_string. Keep the document's own style and its amendment convention: GDDs mark changed rules with an italic "*(Amended 2026-10-29 — ADR-00NN sync)*" note (match the exact format of existing "(Amended 2026-10-2x — ...)" notes in that file).`

const results = await pipeline(args,
  (p) => agent(`${RULES}

Target file: ${p.file}
Work packet (JSON): ${p.packet}. Read it. It has:
- items: ADR-mandated text changes {id, adr, where, old, new, check}. check=OLD_PRESENT: the old text is in the file; replace it with the new text's intent (exact "new" wording where it fits the sentence; adjust grammar to fit). check=old_absent: the exact old text is gone (rewritten since); find the current passage that still carries the old meaning and apply the change, or report already_done if the file already says what "new" says. "where" may name several files; apply only the part that belongs to this file.
- contradictions: requirements in this file that contradict a Proposed ADR. User ruling: the ADR wins. Rewrite the rule (and its acceptance criteria, formulas, tables and edge cases that restate it — grep the rule id and key terms) so it agrees with the ADR. Read the cited ADR section first (docs/architecture/adr-NNNN-*.md, grep then range read). EXCEPTION: if following the ADR would remove or change player-facing design intent that the ADR does not itself address (a feel, a fairness guarantee, a visible behaviour), do NOT edit that row; return it under objections with gdd_intent, adr_says and your recommendation.
- extra (architecture.md only): further stale-text and missing-content tasks; apply each.
After each change, grep the file for other occurrences of the same stale term (old signature, old constant, old priority numbers, "in BOOTING" for content loads, "restore() returns (ok", etc.) and fix those too, listing them under applied.
If a change implies a registry value change (a constant's value, a formula, an interface signature recorded in a registry), add a registry_request naming registry, entry and change.
Return: applied (id + where = section/line + note), already_done, objections, skipped (with reason), registry_requests.`,
    { label: `write:${p.file.split('/').pop()}`, phase: 'Write', schema: WRITE_SCHEMA, model: 'sonnet', effort: 'high' }),
  (w, p) => w ? agent(`${RULES} For this verification step you MAY run exactly one read-only command: git diff -- ${p.file}

Verify the sync edits just made to ${p.file}. Packet: ${p.packet}. Writer report: ${JSON.stringify(w).slice(0, 6000)}
Check, using the diff and targeted reads: (1) each applied change says what the packet's "new" / the ADR says, and the sentence still reads correctly; (2) no unrelated text was deleted or garbled, no table was broken (column counts), no duplicate amendment notes; (3) no stale term the packet targets remains elsewhere in the file (grep). Fix small mechanical problems yourself in ${p.file} (list them in fixed). Report anything you could not fix as problems. ok = no unfixed problems.`,
