{'adr': 'ADR-0017', 'verdict': 'CONCERNS. The core decisions hold up: one block and one hook, a pure integer kernel, a Q14/Q28 cone test, a doubled-coordinate slab segment test, a canonical hysteresis bit, an oracle-proven pair cache, counted work, and pending-intent columns. They are probed on the 4.7.2 editor binary and the alternatives are genuinely weighed. The problem statement, rationale and Consequences are honest, including the admission in D12.6 that the budget does not fit. What passes: the engine is stamped 4.7.2 with Knowledge Risk HIGH, post-cutoff APIs are listed and the probes are dated. All 57 TR rows that requirements-traceability.md maps to ADR-0017 are addressed, and all 46 TR-emitter rows are classified (42 decided, 15 shared, 9 owned elsewhere). Fault domain 6 matches the registry table. The rects_in / QueryBuffer.spans / version_epoch / changed_region usage matches ADR-0014 sections 5 and 6. The hook edges match ADR-0012 and ADR-0015, and the restore_after list matches ADR-0016 position 6. ADR-0004 lets each block number its own key kinds, so kind 1 is legal. Three blocking problems remain. (T1) The save-equivalence (G4) argument for post_restore is incomplete. Hooks ordered after emitter.place can write upstream blocks on the snapshot tick, for example Interaction calling Graph M6 (salvage_node). Re-running the hysteresis in post_restore can then rewrite a canonical lit bit. (T2) D10.2 has registrants re-register (E6) from their own post_restore. That is a cross-block canonical write, which ADR-0016 section 4.2 forbids. (T3) A PLAYER-origin set_switch has no reach, NOT_LIT or loco_state check anywhere on the authoritative path. Beyond those, several cross-ADR assumptions do not match the sibling drafts on disk: M14 is not allowed in the INIT scope, BODY_COORD_LIMIT does not exist, there is no node_base read and no template emitter-placement accessor, ControllerView names differ, and the published occ_origin is missing. These need amendment rows, not redesign.', 'blocking': 3, 'total': 16}
{'adr': 'ADR-0018', 'verdict': 'ACCEPT-WITH-AMENDMENTS. No blocking engine finding. The GDScript in the ADR compiles and runs on 4.7.2 (headless probe, below). No deprecated or post-cutoff API is used, and none of the 4.7 hazards you listed (typed-return override, packed-array element-write setters, Object-method name clashes, const of a constructed object) occurs. The weak point is the engine evidence for rung B. The "probed" claims are plausible: the five scratch scripts p1.gd to p5.gd exist, and their contents match P-1 to P-12 (size_2d_override, snap_2d, PCF13, CULL_CLOCKWISE). But P-9 and P-10 used a 1200x1200 centred light texture, so they never exercise the engine\'s culling of occluders by the light\'s quad extent (E2). The Compatibility cross-check could not be confirmed from the scripts (p1 to p5 contain no compat strings), so treat it as the ADR\'s word. The headless probe I ran covered API shape only; drawing behaviour is UNVERIFIED because headless uses the dummy renderer. Probe sources are in C:/Users/dasbl/AppData/Local/Temp/claude/adr-probes/review-ADR-0018/.', 'blocking': 0, 'total': 6}
{'adr': 'ADR-0018', 'verdict': "CONCERNS. The decision itself is sound and the spike gating is stated honestly. The problem statement is clear, with the parallax arithmetic and the two frame facts. The eight alternatives were genuinely weighed. The negative consequences are stated, including the two-origin model, the fractional-position exception and the single-machine evidence. The engine is stamped as Godot 4.7.2, and the domain is marked HIGH risk with every engine claim probed or UNVERIFIED. TR-light-015 is decided here, and the owned-elsewhere rows are mapped. Three things must be amended before Accept, but none needs a rewrite. First, the lattice exception contradicts three registry stances and two live TR rows, and nothing syncs them. Second, the boot combination invariant in section 6 depends on an Emitter tuning row that the now-written ADR-0017 does not have, and the check is vacuous at ship. Third, no one owns the beam Light2D's parent across BODY_TRANSFER, and the identity contract fails on a pod hop or a weld rebase. The non-blocking items cover sibling wording, an evidence gap in the probes that support rung B, and right-sizing.", 'blocking': 3, 'total': 6}
